.TH TNT-MODULES 7 "2026-08-18" "tnt-modules 0.3.0"
.SH NAME
tnt\-modules \- bundled chat commands for TNT
.SH SYNOPSIS
.EX
\fBTNT_MODULE_PATHS=\fP\fImodule-dir\fP\
[\fB:\fP\fImodule-dir\fP]...\ \fBtnt\fP
.EE
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B tnt\-modules
supplies five optional external-process modules for
.BR tnt (8).
TNT starts each enabled module as a separate child process.
The bundled modules receive public chat messages.
A matching module may publish a public reply under its module name.
Several modules can be enabled together.
Other commands and normal conversation produce no bundled reply.
.SS Bundled commands
Command names are case-sensitive.
Each command must begin the message.
It must end the message or be followed by an ASCII space.
.TP
.BI "/roll [" count "]d" sides "[+/\-" modifier "]"
Roll dice and publish the individual rolls and total.
With no dice expression, roll one six-sided die.
The letter
.B d
is case-insensitive.
.I count
must be 1\(en20,
.I sides
must be 2\(en1000, and the absolute value of
.I modifier
must not exceed 10000.
An invalid expression produces a short usage reply.
.TP
.B /flip
Publish either heads or tails.
Trailing text is ignored.
.TP
.BI "/8ball [" question "]"
Publish one of the 20 classic Magic 8-Ball answers.
The question is optional.
.TP
.BI "/choose " option " | " option " [| " option ]...
Publish one randomly selected pipe-separated option.
Leading and trailing spaces and tabs are removed from each option.
At least two nonempty options are required;
otherwise the module produces a short usage reply.
.TP
.B /quote
Publish a random proverb from the module's built-in list.
Trailing text is ignored.
.SH SECURITY
Module processes run as the TNT service account.
They inherit its user, group, filesystem, and network privileges.
They are not an operating-system sandbox.
Manifest permissions describe protocol capabilities;
they do not restrict system calls or resource access.
.PP
The bundled modules perform no network operations.
Review any replacement or additional module before enabling it,
keep its directory from being writable by untrusted users,
and run TNT under a dedicated least-privileged account.
Use an operating-system sandbox or container for stronger isolation.
.PP
TNT can disable a module that stalls or violates the protocol.
That supervision does not contain filesystem or network side effects.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B TNT_MODULE_PATHS
A colon-separated list of module directories read when TNT starts.
Each directory must contain
.I tnt\-module.json
and the executable entrypoint named by that manifest.
An empty or unset value disables external modules.
TNT enables at most eight valid modules.
Restart TNT after changing the value.
.IP
Relative directories are resolved from TNT's working directory.
Use absolute directories in service configuration.
For the default
.B make install
layout,
enable the dice and coin modules with:
.EX
TNT_MODULE_PATHS=/usr/local/libexec/tnt/modules/roll\-module:\e
/usr/local/libexec/tnt/modules/flip\-module \e
    tnt
.EE
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /usr/local/libexec/tnt/modules/
Default installation root for the five module directories.
Changing
.B PREFIX
or
.B MODULEDIR
when running
.B make install
changes this path.
.TP
.I modules/
Contains the bundled
.BR 8ball\-module ,
.BR choose\-module ,
.BR flip\-module ,
.BR quote\-module ,
and
.B roll\-module
directories in the source distribution.
Pass an individual child directory, not this parent directory, in
.BR TNT_MODULE_PATHS .
Each child contains its manifest, executable entrypoint, JSON parser, and
module program.
.SH VERSIONS
The bundled manifests require TNT 1.1.0 or later and use the
.B tnt.module.v1
protocol.
TNT checks the manifest protocol and startup handshake.
It enables only modules that pass both checks.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tntctl (1),
.BR tnt\-module\-protocol (7),
.BR tnt (8)
